3. `home-manager.extraSpecialArgs = { inherit inputs; };` — so home/kasiro can access inputs. Wait — actually simpler alternative: import the HM module not inside home/kasiro but at the nixos level in flake.nix inside the modules list:

```nix
{
  home-manager.users.kasiro = { imports = [ hermes-agent.homeManagerModules.default ]; } // import ./home/kasiro;
}
```
Hmm messy. Cleaner: extraSpecialArgs. But note: home-manager.users.kasiro = import ./home/kasiro — a module function {pkgs,...}:. With extraSpecialArgs = {inherit inputs;}, the function head can take inputs. But extraSpecialArgs applies to both users — fine.

Wait, actually there's an even cleaner route: in the modules list of nixosSystem, we have `hermes-agent` in scope (outputs arg). We could do:

```nix
home-manager.users.kasiro = {
  imports = [ hermes-agent.homeManagerModules.default ];
  ...
};
```
But home/kasiro is imported separately. Could merge: `home-manager.users.kasiro = { imports = [ hermes-agent.homeManagerModules.default ]; } // import ./home/kasiro;` — no, // on module function vs attrset doesn't work.

Best: extraSpecialArgs. Add in the anonymous module:
```nix
home-manager.extraSpecialArgs = { inherit inputs; };
```
specialArgs at nixosSystem already has inputs but HM doesn't inherit those automatically (HM nixos module passes its own args: pkgs, config, plus extraSpecialArgs). Actually home-manager.nixosModules.home-manager does pass through the module args? I believe the HM nixos module uses the nixos module system's args for the HM modules it defines... Not certain — safer to use extraSpecialArgs explicitly. That's the documented approach.

Then in home/kasiro/default.nix: change function head to `{ pkgs, inputs, ... }:` and add `inputs.hermes-agent.homeManagerModules.default` to imports, or create separate hermes.nix file with head `{ inputs, ... }:`. New file needs git add — fine, I'll delegate editing to general and instruct git add.

